Dave,

My thoughts on Thierry's email. These are my opinions and advice, take what you need for the different forums.

The high level steer first. Four things stand out to me.

1. He is reviewing at the wrong level. This is a direction and priorities document, and almost all of his questions are delivery and solutioning questions. Migration plans, run costs, historical data handling all belong at the feature brief stage, and the workflow in the paper already routes them there. My advice is to reset this gently but clearly: the paper sets direction, the feature briefs carry the detail, and the detail he is asking about is planned for exactly that stage.
2. He is right to question capacity, and we have already asked those questions. We raised the capacity concern ourselves, and Techreative have told us what they can deliver. Holding them to that is the piece LSV has historically been soft on, and it sits squarely with him. I would welcome him owning it and bringing an answer with a date.
3. His assessment only covers half the picture, and the other half is already in the paper. He has rated risk and effort, which tell us what a feature costs, but nothing about what it returns. The impact of each feature is clearly set out in the document, and his review does not engage with it. Risk and effort ratings on their own cannot prioritise anything, they can only say no. My advice is to point him back to the impact already described against each feature, because that is the half of the model his review skipped. It also answers his question about criteria weights, since weights only mean something once benefit is part of the picture.
4. The timing concerns me, as we discussed. He has had the document for over a month, the content was consulted and settled, and this reopens settled ground while he is also telling us time is short before season. My advice for Thursday is to keep the focus on next steps, ELT noting, feature briefs starting, and the capacity answer, rather than reopening settled content.

On the AI comments, since you asked for my steer. This is my area, so I will be direct.

My view is that the broad "our risk appetite isn't high" comment is a lazy frame and poor advice. I would suggest there is a higher risk in sticking our head in the sand than in anything the paper proposes. Everything in it is deliberately small, assistive and reversible. The AI Brief summarises a page the operator already has open, delegates no decisions to a model, and can be switched off cleanly. His own ratings agree with this, he scored it low risk and low effort, then argued against it at the theme level.

Three reasons the do-nothing path carries the higher risk.

1. The cost of the status quo is real and recurring. Volunteers spend hours every season summarising, re-keying and assembling reports, and we pay that cost again every season we do nothing.
2. People are already using AI tools informally. A controlled programme, with costs, controls and rollback documented in each feature brief, is the risk management, not the risk.
3. It runs against our strategic plan, which is to invest in technology and AI. Declining small, controlled initiatives now means doing our first AI work later, under pressure, once it has become a requirement. That is the higher risk version.

One more on the AI comments. His suggestion to focus on UX, templates and workflows instead of using AI to fix bad data actually describes what the paper proposes. The AI assistance sits at the point of capture, prompting for missing information and mapping plain language into structured fields. Nobody is proposing to clean bad data after the fact. My read is he has not had the context yet, and sharing the design intent will close this one quickly.

Quick answers on his specific points if useful. Categorisation migration is planned, feature brief detail. Secondary forms move to structured data going forward, with little value in backdating. The incident labels comment misreads the design intent, there is more context behind the approach. The native app and rostering questions both have history that should be understood before they are reopened. PDF reporting serves operational and interagency needs, suggest sharing the LIMSOC Reporting Approach documents. Implied access does require software changes.

Call me if you want to run through any of it.

